---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { useTranslations, absoluteUrl, localePath } from '../i18n/utils';

const lang = 'en';
const t = useTranslations(lang);
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${t('nav.about')} - ${t('site.title')}`}
  description={t('about.description')}
  lang={lang}
  alternateJa={absoluteUrl('/about/', 'ja')}
  ogImage="/og/about.png"
>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>{t('about.heading')}</h1>

    <h2>Mission</h2>
    <p>{t('about.mission')}</p>

    <h2>Origin</h2>
    <p>
      Bitcoin Institute began as the companion archive to
      <a href={localePath('/novel/', lang)}><em>Genesis: The Disappearance of the Founder and the Promise</em></a>,
      a work of historical fiction. In the novel, Misaki establishes the Bitcoin Institute.
      The archive you are reading is a real open-source project: it preserves primary records
      and publishes editorial readings based on them.
    </p>
    <p>
      The novel's Misaki is the fictional founder and the source of the archive's editorial
      voice. This is a relationship between a work of fiction and a public project, not a claim
      that Misaki is a real researcher or author.
    </p>

    <h2>Scope</h2>

    <h3>Primary sources</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Satoshi Nakamoto's writings</strong> — All emails, forum posts, and code comments</li>
      <li><strong>Conversations</strong> — Exchanges with Adam Back, Wei Dai, Hal Finney, James A. Donald, and others</li>
      <li><strong>Self-published web documents</strong> — A participant's own self-published web writings and standalone site pages, preserved verbatim — e.g. Nick Szabo's bit gold, Wei Dai's b-money</li>
      <li><strong>Bitcoin Improvement Proposals (BIPs)</strong> — Key protocol specifications that shaped Bitcoin's development, including HD wallets, SegWit, Taproot, and more</li>
      <li><strong>Tweets (X / Twitter)</strong> — Historically significant X (formerly Twitter) posts archived verbatim with the original handle. Edited commentary about each tweet lives in a separate editorial article that links to the primary tweet entry.</li>
      <li><strong>Court documents</strong> — Trial evidence and testimony, e.g. the COPA v Wright case</li>
      <li><strong>The Bitcoin whitepaper</strong> — Both the October 31, 2008 final PDF and its October 3, 2008 pre-release draft, archived verbatim</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Editorial readings</h3>
    <p>Bitcoin Institute's own writing about the archive's material, in five forms:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Articles</strong> — Read a primary record, event, or multi-year incident in the archive: retrospectives, media coverage, and key historical accounts from Bitcoin's early history</li>
      <li><strong>Analyses</strong> — Develop interpretive arguments across multiple sources</li>
      <li><strong>Biographies</strong> — Compile a person's documented history</li>
      <li><strong>Design documents</strong> — Walk through a specific mechanism in Bitcoin's protocol design, from the system overview down to individual subsystems such as consensus, monetary policy, cryptography, and storage</li>
      <li><strong>Currencies</strong> — Profile a single altcoin or stablecoin's protocol design — supply, consensus, governance, initial distribution — read against Bitcoin as the baseline</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Every form listed here — primary sources and editorial readings alike — can be browsed from the header's Browse menu, under <a href={localePath('/types/', lang)}>By Type</a>.</p>

    <h2>Policy</h2>

    <h3>Core principles</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No editorial voice in primary entries</strong> — Primary entries (emails, forum posts, BIPs, court records) preserve the source content verbatim, with no editorial speculation or interpretation added</li>
      <li><strong>Interpretation in editorial readings</strong> — Editorial readings are explicitly labeled as interpretation, with each claim cited to its source</li>
      <li><strong>Sources required</strong> — Every entry must cite its original source with a verifiable URL</li>
      <li><strong>Accuracy</strong> — Content is verified against original archives</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Detailed rules</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Content preservation</strong> — Once archived, content is permanently retained even if the original source becomes unavailable. Source URLs reflect the location at the time of archival and may no longer be accessible.</li>
      <li><strong>Multiple sources</strong> — Where possible, entries cite secondary sources (e.g., Satoshi Nakamoto Institute) to provide redundancy against link rot.</li>
      <li><strong>Timestamps</strong> — All dates and times are in UTC. Timestamps from the cryptography mailing list (displayed in EDT/EST by the Mailman archiver) have been converted to UTC. BitcoinTalk forum timestamps are UTC by default. Where only a date is known (e.g., web documents, court proceedings), the time is recorded as 00:00:00 UTC. The Satoshi Nakamoto Institute's UTC-normalized timestamps are used as the primary reference.</li>
      <li><strong>Names in translation</strong> — In the Japanese edition, person names are normalized to katakana in UI and editorial text for consistency. Quotes, source titles, organization names, brand names, handles, and URLs retain their original-language forms where appropriate.</li>
      <li><strong>Entry granularity</strong> — Each primary source document (email, forum post, letter, or court record) is archived as a single entry. One document equals one entry. Editorial entries range in scope from a single event (an article) to a cross-source argument (an analysis) or an entire subsystem (a design document).</li>
      <li><strong>Thread structure</strong> — Related messages are grouped into threads. Each message remains an individual entry, linked by a shared thread identifier. Figures listed on each entry reflect only the direct conversation partners (author and respondent), not all thread figures.</li>
      <li><strong>Biographies</strong> — Biographical profiles of key figures are compiled from multiple public sources and displayed on each figure's page. All facts cited in biographies are backed by verifiable source URLs listed in the entry's references.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <p>Content is sourced from publicly available archives:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="https://www.metzdowd.com/pipermail/cryptography/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cryptography Mailing List (metzdowd.com)</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://mailing-list-archive.cryptoanarchy.wiki/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cypherpunks Mailing List Archive (cryptoanarchy.wiki)</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://sourceforge.net/p/bitcoin/mailman/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bitcoin-list Mailing List (sourceforge.net)</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://bitcointalk.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BitcoinTalk Forum</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/2015/http://p2pfoundation.ning.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">P2P Foundation (Wayback Machine; original offline)</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://sourceforge.net/projects/bitcoin/files/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SourceForge Bitcoin Releases</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bitcoin Whitepaper (bitcoin.org)</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://mmalmi.github.io/satoshi/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Martti Malmi's Satoshi Email Archive</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://gwern.net/doc/bitcoin/2008-nakamoto" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gwern's Archive (Wei Dai / Satoshi Nakamoto emails)</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://bitcoinmagazine.com/technical/bitcoin-adam-backs-complete-emails-satoshi-nakamoto" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bitcoin Magazine (Adam Back / Satoshi Nakamoto emails)</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://plan99.net/~mike/satoshi-emails/thread1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mike Hearn's Published Satoshi Emails (plan99.net)</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.opencrypto.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">COPA v Wright Trial Evidence</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/bitcoin/bips" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bitcoin Improvement Proposals (BIPs)</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://satoshi.nakamotoinstitute.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Satoshi Nakamoto Institute</a> (secondary reference)</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Citations</h2>
    <p>
      For the recommended citation format and the fixed-source map for technical readings, see
      <a href={localePath('/cite/', lang)}>How to cite this archive</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Contributing</h2>
    <p>
      This is an open source project. Contributions are welcome via
      <a href="https://github.com/cypher256/BitcoinArchive" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* This page has no .entry-content/.biography-content wrapper, so it
     falls outside the shared markdown-body rule. Same two shared
     tokens as every other heading-underline consumer, just its own
     selector — the plain h2 rule stays bare so it does not collide
     with .section-header on the home page. */
  h2 {
    padding-bottom: var(--heading-underline-gap);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border-strong);
  }
</style>
